2026-01-31 21:38:41 | OUTPUT |     
2026-01-31 21:38:41 | OUTPUT |     For SURPRISE & SETUP: Both have good misdirection, but Joke 2’s setup ("deeply considered, ethically driven choice") more effectively primes the audience for a lofty justification, making the crass electoral calculation a bigger, funnier drop. Joke 1’s twist from "disappointing policies" to criticizing recycling commitment is unexpected, but the target (Green Party idealism) is a slightly more predictable satirical angle.
2026-01-31 21:38:41 | OUTPUT |     
2026-01-31 21:38:41 | OUTPUT |     ECONOMY: Joke 2 is tighter. The setup questions, while detailed, efficiently build the "principled stand" facade. Joke 1’s setup is slightly more verbose ("issued a rather scathing condemnation..."), and the punchline’s delivery ("is frankly, *optimistic*") has a slightly more discursive feel.
2026-01-31 21:38:41 | OUTPUT |     
2026-01-31 21:38:41 | OUTPUT |     HEADLINE RELEVANCE: Both are perfectly relevant, specifically about an ex-Labour MP defecting to the Greens and condemning Starmer. Joke 1 focuses on the fickleness of the defector (already planning the next defection). Joke 2 focuses on the cynical, non-ideological motive behind the defection and condemnation.
2026-01-31 21:38:41 | OUTPUT |     
